                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "e480b47a-9c9d-3812-9d10-9471447396a8",
                        "entry_id": "26d70576-4da4-36e1-b52a-499a70ce2829",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Qui se rapporte à la théorie de Keynes."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "3ec425f0-7ad5-4bf7-874f-6d3f1c3f7cdc",
                                "sense_id": "e480b47a-9c9d-3812-9d10-9471447396a8",
                                "text": "À l'automne 1975, pour faire face au choc pétrolier, Jacques Chirac, Premier ministre, annonce un plan de relance de la croissance keynésien (hausse des aides sociales, baisses des impôts).."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "c2960ff4-1381-44bd-a191-f73f8a2ae345",
                                "sense_id": "e480b47a-9c9d-3812-9d10-9471447396a8",
                                "text": "Le gouvernement britannique opte pour une relance keynésienne."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "0b88be0a-1620-45d4-8cd0-b4aa4648e13f",
                                "sense_id": "e480b47a-9c9d-3812-9d10-9471447396a8",
                                "text": "Contrairement à ce qu’on pense souvent, la gauche était assez largement dominante jusqu’en 1970-75, pendant la période des Trente Glorieuses : elle était dominante sous sa forme interventionniste, keynésienne, progressiste."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "14154d6d-6c4e-43ae-a0d2-202a7bb1a635",
                        "source_entry_id": "26d70576-4da4-36e1-b52a-499a70ce2829",
                        "source_sense_id": "e480b47a-9c9d-3812-9d10-9471447396a8",
                        "target_lang_word": "Keynesian",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "Keynesian",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "d10b7649-16eb-399b-942d-5044efb95dcc",
                                        "entry_id": "d4dfbf2e-223d-3dc0-a547-fa2cdcae25d2",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Of or pertaining to an economic theory based on the ideas of John Maynard Keynes, as put forward in his book The General Theory of Employment, Interest and Money, published in 1936 in response to the Great Depression of the 1930s, and extensively extended by a large body of followers before and after his death in 1946."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ab7fa32c-bcac-4495-95ad-5dc76c37157e",
                                                "sense_id": "d10b7649-16eb-399b-942d-5044efb95dcc",
                                                "text": "That’s why supply and demand get out of balance in a slump, according to the so-called New Keynesian model that Fed officials and other policy makers lean on."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "Keynesian",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "1515bf7a-e739-328d-a068-9cb567fd0574",
                                        "entry_id": "bfc5fce6-ea32-3e84-95bb-fe655a4cade1",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A proponent of Keynesian economic doctrine."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "fa4a268b-3c69-441d-a14d-9acf44d824bb",
                                                "sense_id": "1515bf7a-e739-328d-a068-9cb567fd0574",
                                                "text": "We Are All Keynesians Now [title]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "14e22fe3-6dc8-434e-a362-e744cf57ba1a",
                                                "sense_id": "1515bf7a-e739-328d-a068-9cb567fd0574",
                                                "text": "If I'd believed in all their policies, [...] I would've been a Keynesian and a Friedmanite, a grammar school preserver and destroyer, a nationalisation freak and a privatisation maniac; but above all, I would have been a stark-staring raving schizophrenic! (laugh track)."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "80458833-66ae-43e3-be48-844429ddaa30",
                                                "sense_id": "1515bf7a-e739-328d-a068-9cb567fd0574",
                                                "text": "Since the 1960's the Keynesians have also come under heavy criticism by a group of economists known as Neo-Keynesians. The main contention of the Neo-Keynesians is that the Keynesians have no adequate theory on inflation."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "fbfe1a9f-847e-4a1e-a261-4db45d07efb0",
                        "source_entry_id": "26d70576-4da4-36e1-b52a-499a70ce2829",
                        "source_sense_id": "e480b47a-9c9d-3812-9d10-9471447396a8",
